voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer","b":"gemini:Kore | bright, curious"}
prompt: "The Riemann hypothesis, explained step by step for a curious adult with school maths. Start from the primes and how irregular they look, then Gauss's guess for how many primes there are below a number, then Euler's link between the primes and an infinite sum, then Riemann's zeta function and its zeros, the critical line where every zero found so far lies, and how that line controls the errors in counting primes. End with why it matters (a million-dollar prize, cryptography, much of number theory assumes it) and what is still open."

## The Riemann Hypothesis
domain: {"x":[0,1080],"y":[0,200]}
vars: {"N":0}
sources:
    - id: riemann
      title: Ueber die Anzahl der Primzahlen unter einer gegebenen Grösse
      authors: Bernhard Riemann
      year: 1859
      finding: zeta's zeros control the prime count
    - id: hardy
      title: Sur les zéros de la fonction ζ(s) de Riemann
      authors: G. H. Hardy
      year: 1914
      finding: infinitely many zeros on the line
    - id: conrey
      title: More than two fifths of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function are on the critical line
      authors: J. B. Conrey
      year: 1989
      finding: at least two in five on the line
    - id: gourdon
      title: The 10^13 first zeros of the Riemann zeta function, and zeros computation at very large height
      authors: Xavier Gourdon
      year: 2004
      finding: 10^13 zeros, all on the line
    - id: schoenfeld
      title: Sharper bounds for the Chebyshev functions θ(x) and ψ(x). II
      authors: Lowell Schoenfeld
      year: 1976
      finding: error below √x·ln x/8π, if RH holds

