    curve band_hi dashed #2f6b8f expr 0.5772+ln(ln(x))+ln(x)+ln(x)^2/4+ln(x)^3/18+ln(x)^4/96+ln(x)^5/600+ln(x)^6/4320+ln(x)^7/35280+ln(x)^8/322560+ln(x)^9/3265920+ln(x)^10/36288000+ln(x)^11/439084800+ln(x)^12/5748019200+ln(x)^13/80951270400+ln(x)^14/1220496076800+ln(x)^15/19615115520000+ln(x)^16/334764638208000+ln(x)^17/6046686277632000+ln(x)^18/115242726703104000+sqrt(x)*ln(x)/8 x_from 100 x_to 1000 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    curve band_lo dashed #2f6b8f expr 0.5772+ln(ln(x))+ln(x)+ln(x)^2/4+ln(x)^3/18+ln(x)^4/96+ln(x)^5/600+ln(x)^6/4320+ln(x)^7/35280+ln(x)^8/322560+ln(x)^9/3265920+ln(x)^10/36288000+ln(x)^11/439084800+ln(x)^12/5748019200+ln(x)^13/80951270400+ln(x)^14/1220496076800+ln(x)^15/19615115520000+ln(x)^16/334764638208000+ln(x)^17/6046686277632000+ln(x)^18/115242726703104000-sqrt(x)*ln(x)/8 x_from 100 x_to 1000 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    region band x_from 100 x_to 1000 cites ["schoenfeld"] details "Drawn to its shape only. Assuming the Riemann hypothesis, Schoenfeld (1976) proved $$|\\pi(x) - \\mathrm{li}(x)| < \\frac{\\sqrt{x}\\,\\ln x}{8\\pi} \\quad (x \\ge 2657).$$" between ["band_lo","band_hi"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.15 hidden true
    label lab_band above #2f6b8f attach_to band_hi tex "\\pm\\sqrt{x}\\,\\ln x" hidden true
    mark box_band around lab_band kind box hidden true

    card title "The Riemann Hypothesis"

    camera center.data [60,18] zoom 4.5 duration 0.4

How many primes are there below a hundred, or below a million, and is there any rule to where they fall?
    draw ax

A prime has no divisors but one and itself: two, three, five, seven, eleven.
    draw stair1

    pause 0.3

Look at the steps, and you see bursts and droughts.
    blocking false

    point at.data [12,5] gesture circle duration 1.4

    point at.data [93,24] gesture underline duration 1.6

Most people would conclude the primes  (@draw r100@)are simply chaos, with no law at all.
    highlight stair1 effect pulse

    erase r100

The way out runs through three ideas: Gauss's guess, Euler's sum, and Riemann's zeros.
    camera reset true duration 2

Start with what you see from far away: the jagged steps begin to look like a smooth climb.
    draw stair2 r1000

    pause 0.3

    erase r1000

Around 1792 a teenage Gauss guessed why: near a number x, about one number in every ln x is prime.
    draw dens

So primes thin out slowly, and adding up that thinning chance gives his smooth curve.
    draw li lab_li_curve

At a thousand, his curve predicts about 178 primes, while the true count is 168.
    draw p_li lab_li p_true lab_true

    erase dens

That the curve is right on average was proved in 1896, so the real ques (@highlight err duration 2@)tion is the error: how far can the steps stray?
    draw err

    pause 0.4

To measure that error, you need a grip on all the primes at once, and Euler had found one long before: an infinite sum tied to the primes.
    draw card_bg eq_sum

Every whole number is built from primes in exactly one way, so that sum equals a product w (@highlight eq_prod part 2^{-s} duration 0.8@)ith one fact (@highlight eq_prod part 3^{-s} duration 0.8@)or for each  (@highlight eq_prod part 5^{-s} duration 0.8@)prime.
    draw eq_prod

    erase eq_prod

In 1859 Riemann took Euler's sum one step further: he let s be a complex number, a point on a plane rather than a line, and called the sum zeta.
    morph eq_sum tex "\\zeta(s) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^s}" duration 2

    erase card_bg eq_sum

The secret lies in the points where zeta equals zero.
    draw plane_bg re_axis im_axis lab_re lab_im

Apart from easy zeros at minus two, minus four and so on,
    draw triv1 triv2 triv3 lab_triv

every zero lies in a narrow strip.
    draw strip lab0 lab1

Riemann noticed the first ones sitting exactly on the line down the middle, where the  (@draw z1 lab_z1@)real part is one half.
    draw crit lab_crit

His hypothesis is that every one of them lies on that line.
    draw z2 lab_z2 z3 lab_z3 z4 z5 z6 z7 z8

More than ten trillion have been checked, and every one is on it.
    draw checked

    pause 0.4

We started with counting primes and have ended up with dots on a plane. So why should the zeros of a sum care about counting primes?
    highlight zeros_all effect pulse

    erase plane

Riemann found an exact formula in which each zero adds one wave to Gauss's smooth curve.
    draw waves counter

Add the waves one zero at a time, and the smooth curve bends into the staircase itself.
    animate N 30 duration 7 easing linear

    pause 0.5

    fade waves to 0.15 duration 0.8

    erase counter

A zero with real part one half makes a wave that grows only like the square root of x.
    draw band_hi band_lo band lab_band parallel true

So if the hypothesis holds, the error stays within roughly the square root of x, times a logarithm: as small as it could possibly be.
    blocking false

    highlight stair duration 3

    highlight err duration 2.5

That is why it carries one of the Clay Institute's million-dollar prizes, and why countless theorems begin "assume the Riemann hypothesis."
    focus band band_hi band_lo lab_band stair

You may have heard it linked to cryptography, which runs on huge primes. Yet a proof would break no code; it would tell us more surely where primes hide.
    highlight stair

Hardy proved that infinitely many zeros lie on the line, and we now know at least two in five do, but all of them, no one has shown.
    highlight band_hi band_lo effect pulse

So the primes are wild one at a time, yet their count clings to Gauss's curve, and the zeros of zeta decide how tightly; the hypothesis say (@draw box_band ends@)s as tightly as possible.
    highlight stair li band delivery grave

    quiz "Suppose someone found a zeta zero with real part 0.8, off the critical line. What would that mean for counting primes?"
        right: The count would still follow Gauss's curve on average, but that zero's wave would grow like x to the 0.8, so the error could grow far beyond the square root of x.
        wrong: Each zero adds a wave to Gauss's curve, and that wave grows like x raised to the zero's real part. What happens to a wave that grows like x to the 0.8 instead of x to the one half?
        * The primes would run out
        + Still Gauss's curve on average, but errors far beyond √x
        * Gauss's curve would become exact
        * Nothing: zeros don't affect the count

Twenty-five primes below a hundred, and for every number beyond, one unproved line would tell us how close Gauss's guess must come.
    camera center.data [60,18] zoom 4.5 duration 2.5

