      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:c59voc'
    '|a||Every whole number is built from primes in exactly one way, so that sum equals a product with one factor for each prime.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1kwqpr3'
    '|a||In 1859 Riemann took Euler''s sum one step further: he let s be a complex number, a point on a plane rather than a line, and called the sum zeta.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:lvn932'
    '|a||The secret lies in the points where zeta equals zero.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:dkvsak'
    '|a||Apart from easy zeros at minus two, minus four and so on,':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1nlh1g9'
    '|a||every zero lies in a narrow strip.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1rth30a'
    '|a||Riemann noticed the first ones sitting exactly on the line down the middle, where the real part is one half.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1gysa7h'
    '|a||His hypothesis is that every one of them lies on that line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1syo0tw'
    '|a||More than ten trillion have been checked, and every one is on it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:153s4y4'
    '|a||We started with counting primes and have ended up with dots on a plane. So why should the zeros of a sum care about counting primes?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:sg725n'
    '|a||Riemann found an exact formula in which each zero adds one wave to Gauss''s smooth curve.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:13kse32'
    '|a||Add the waves one zero at a time, and the smooth curve bends into the staircase itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:zetoev'
    '|a||A zero with real part one half makes a wave that grows only like the square root of x.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:vbzool'
    '|a||So if the hypothesis holds, the error stays within roughly the square root of x, times a logarithm: as small as it could possibly be.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:2kg2qz'
    '|a||That is why it carries one of the Clay Institute''s million-dollar prizes, and why countless theorems begin "assume the Riemann hypothesis."':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1rc4wfk'
    '|a||You may have heard it linked to cryptography, which runs on huge primes. Yet a proof would break no code; it would tell us more surely where primes hide.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:mug2a1'
    '|a||Hardy proved that infinitely many zeros lie on the line, and we now know at least two in five do, but all of them, no one has shown.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:8s67sb'
    '|a|grave|So the primes are wild one at a time, yet their count clings to Gauss''s curve, and the zeros of zeta decide how tightly; the hypothesis says as tightly as possible.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1mm08p7'
    '|a||Suppose someone found a zeta zero with real part 0.8, off the critical line. What would that mean for counting primes?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer #ctx:1odoz15'
    '|a||The count would still follow Gauss''s curve on average, but that zero''s wave would grow like x to the 0.8, so the error could grow far beyond the square root of x.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer
    '|a||Twenty-five primes below a hundred, and for every number beyond, one unproved line would tell us how close Gauss''s guess must come.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer
    '|a||Each zero adds a wave to Gauss''s curve, and that wave grows like x raised to the zero''s real part. What happens to a wave that grows like x to the 0.8 instead of x to the one half?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer
    '|a||Yes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer
    '|a||Right.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: gemini:Charon | clear, warm explainer
    '|a||That''s it.':
      mp3: 
